    <h1 class="sr-spoke-header-title">Igbo Culture</h1>
    <p class="sr-spoke-header-deck">The republic without a king — governance, justice, and cosmology Achebe renders in full before disrupting it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-spoke-content">

    <p class="sr-lede">The world of <em>Things Fall Apart</em> operates according to a complete and coherent system of governance, justice, cosmology, and social obligation. Achebe presents his reader a story about a culture in which anger, strength, and status are embedded in a specific and sophisticated structure of cultural meaning.</p>

    <h2>Political Structure: The Republic Without a King</h2>

    <p>The single most important fact about pre-colonial Igbo political life is that the Igbo had no kings, no paramount chiefs, and no permanent rulers with authority over more than a single village.</p>

    <p>Scholars describe this as an <em>acephalous</em> political system — literally, headless. Each village was a sovereign political unit, governed by a council of all family heads, in which any adult male could speak and major decisions required consensus. Above the family stood no higher individual authority; above the village council stood no higher institution except the ancestral spirits, who spoke through the egwugwu, and the Oracle, whose rulings no one could override.</p>

    <h3>The structural layers of Igbo governance</h3>

    <p><strong>The family (<em>Umunna</em>)</strong> — The basic unit of political and social life. A patrilineage of related families, led by the eldest male — the <em>Okpara</em>, who held the <em>Ofo</em> staff of ancestral authority. The Okpara settled internal family disputes and performed ritual functions on behalf of the family's ancestral lineage.</p>

    <p><strong>The village council (<em>Oha-na-Eze</em>)</strong> — The council of all family heads. This was the highest human governing body, a form of direct democracy in which any adult male could speak, decisions required consensus, and contested rulings could be rejected and renegotiated. The council determined war and peace, adjudicated disputes between families, and maintained community order.</p>

    <p><strong>Age grades</strong> — Cohorts of men born within a few years of each other, who formed peer associations and performed collective labor, civic maintenance, policing, and defense throughout their lives. Age grades cut across family and lineage affiliations, creating community bonds that supplemented family loyalty.</p>

    <p><strong>The Ozo title system</strong> — The highest individual honor in Igbo society. Wealthy men who had demonstrated exceptional ability and community standing could purchase the <em>Ozo</em> title (also called <em>Ichie</em> in some communities) which granted authority to preside over councils and adjudicate disputes. The title was not hereditary. A man's son did not inherit his Ozo standing; he had to earn it himself through demonstrated achievement. This is the meritocratic logic that Okonkwo has internalized completely: a man is what he makes of himself, not what he was born into.</p>

    <p>The consequence for the novel's plot is direct. Okonkwo's father Unoka took no titles, grew no yams, died in debt. In a hereditary aristocracy, Okonkwo would have inherited that diminished status. In Igbo society, he began from nothing and made himself great — which is exactly what drives his consuming ambition and his consuming terror of failure.</p>

