    <h3>Chukwu and Chineke — the Supreme Being</h3>
    <p>The distant, supreme creative force from which all things derive. Chukwu is not directly petitioned or personally accessible; interactions with the divine world happen primarily through lesser spirits and deities. The concept has some analogy to monotheistic traditions, but Igbo theology does not imply a direct personal relationship with Chukwu. Chukwu is the ground of being, not a personal God who intervenes in daily life.</p>

    <h3>Ala — the Earth Goddess</h3>
    <p>The most immediately powerful and morally significant force in Igbo life. Ala governs the land, fertility, and morality. She defines what constitutes an <em>alu</em> — an abomination, a taboo violation. Crimes against Ala are the most serious offenses possible, because they violate the sacred ground on which the entire community depends for its survival. When Okonkwo accidentally kills a clansman's son and is immediately sent into exile, the punishment is not social censure — it is the response the community must make to protect itself from Ala's wrath. The crime is against the earth itself.</p>

    <h3>Chi — the Personal Spirit</h3>
    <p>Every person possesses a <em>chi</em>: a personal spirit assigned at birth that accompanies them through life and shapes their destiny. The chi is neither a deity nor an impersonal force like fate, but something between the two: the personification of what a person is capable of becoming. "When a man says yes, his chi says yes also": personal determination and chi work in alignment. But the chi also sets limits. When Okonkwo reflects that his chi has turned against him, he is confronting the question of how much any person can will their own fate, and how much is determined by forces beyond their choice.</p>

    <p>The chi resists translation. Achebe deliberately leaves it untranslated throughout the novel. Scholars have compared it to the Hindu concept of karma, the Greek <em>daimon</em>, and the Christian soul, but none of these comparisons quite capture this specifically and irreducibly Igbo concept. By refusing to translate it, Achebe refuses to let the idea be absorbed into a simpler framework.</p>

    <h3>Agbala — the Oracle of the Hills and the Caves</h3>
    <p>The Oracle at Awka is the supreme judicial and spiritual authority for Umuofia and the surrounding villages. Its priestess, Chielo, speaks in the god's voice; her rulings carry the weight of divine command. No community goes to war without first consulting the Oracle. The Oracle decides cases that the egwugwu cannot resolve, including whether individuals must die. When the Oracle decrees that Ikemefuna must be killed, the elders of Umuofia act on that decree without question, and Okonkwo participates in the killing despite Obierika's warning that he need not be there.</p>

    <p>The word <em>agbala</em> carries a double meaning: it is the name of the Oracle, and it is also an insult used to describe a man who has taken no titles, meaning that he is without honor, "like a woman." Okonkwo's most cutting contempt is directed at men he calls <em>agbala</em>. The word connects the novel's cosmological and social registers. The Oracle is the source of ultimate authority; the insult is the accusation of ultimate failure. Both derive from the single concept of a man without standing.</p>

