    <h3>The Igbo Week and Sacred Time</h3>
    <p>The Igbo week is four days — Eke, Orie, Afor, Nkwo — each with its own market, its own sacred associations, and its own governing deities. Sacred time structures daily and seasonal life. The Week of Peace — observed before planting season — is an absolute prohibition on all violence and conflict, allowing Ala to bless the coming harvest. When Okonkwo beats his wife during the Week of Peace, the community's alarm has nothing to do with the violence itself. Okonkwo has offended the earth goddess at the moment she is supposed to be welcoming the new season. The entire community's harvest is at risk.</p>

    <p>This is one of the novel's early and crucial signals that in Igbo society, individual behavior and communal welfare are inseparable. What one person does has consequences for everyone, mediated through the cosmological framework.</p>

    <h3>Egwugwu — the Ancestral Court</h3>
    <p>The egwugwu are men of the village who don elaborate masks and costumes to become, for the duration of the ceremony, the ancestral spirits of the dead. The grammar here is precise: they do not represent or perform the ancestors; they become them. When masked, the man inside is gone; only the spirit is present. Women are forbidden from approaching the egwugwu's compound or learning what lies behind the masks.</p>

    <p>The egwugwu constitute the highest court in the land. They hear disputes between families and clans that the village council cannot resolve. Their rulings carry the authority of the ancestral dead — and therefore of the entire accumulated community. When Enoch tears the mask from an egwugwu in the novel's third section, he is not simply committing an insult or a cultural violation. He is killing an ancestor. The egwugwu's response — burning Enoch's compound and the church — follows directly from the logic of what has occurred: a death requires a response.</p>

