    <h2>Gender, Yams, and Social Standing</h2>

    <p>Igbo society is patrilineal and largely patriarchal, with distinct masculine and feminine domains, but the structure is more nuanced than simple gendered subordination.</p>

    <p><strong>Yam cultivation is men's work</strong>, and a man's yam harvest is the primary measure of his standing. Yams are not simply food; they are the currency of social status, the offering made to ancestors, and the symbol of a man's ability to provide. A man who cannot grow yams — as Unoka cannot — has failed at his most fundamental obligation. The novel's first section establishes this through Okonkwo's obsession with acquisition: his barns full of yams are the proof that he is everything his father was not.</p>

    <p><strong>Women's domains</strong> — cooking, managing the household, raising children, tending smaller crops, trading at markets — involve real economic activity and real authority within their sphere. Igbo women were capable of organized political action, as the Women's War of 1929 would demonstrate. But formal authority — titles, council seats, judicial roles — was exclusively male.</p>

    <ImageCaption
      src="/images/summer-reading/tfa/t2-akwete-cloth.jpg"
      alt="Detail of an Akwete woven cloth with geometric patterns in red, black, and ivory, produced by Igbo weavers in Akwete, Nigeria"
      figureNumber={3}
      caption="Akwete cloth, Igbo weavers, Akwete, Rivers State, Nigeria. Akwete is a center of Igbo hand-weaving, producing textiles with complex geometric patterns through a technique unique to the region. Cloth — its production, trade, and gifting — was a significant element of women's economic activity and a carrier of social meaning across Igboland."
      credit="Brooklyn Museum. CC BY 3.0."
      accent="tfa"
