    <p><strong>The bride price (<em>Ika-Akalika</em>)</strong> is the negotiated exchange between families that formalizes a marriage. It is conducted through a ritual exchange of short broomsticks to avoid the vulgarity of naming a price for a person's daughter. The bride price is not the purchase of a woman; it is the establishment of a bond between families and the definition of each side's obligations. Its negotiation, as rendered in the novel during Obierika's daughter's suitor visit, is itself a social form — elaborate, ceremonial, and meaningful.</p>

    <p><strong>The <em>osu</em> — the outcast system.</strong> The <em>osu</em> are people dedicated to the service of a particular deity, or those whose ancestors were so dedicated. Their status is permanent and hereditary; they cannot marry freeborn Igbo, cannot take titles, and are excluded from communal activities. They are not enslaved, but they are ritually separated from the free community. When Christianity arrives and Nwoye's fellow converts include <em>osu</em>, the question of whether freeborn Christians and <em>osu</em> Christians can worship together becomes a genuine crisis. The church's willingness to include the <em>osu</em> is one of the real reasons it attracts Igbo people whom the traditional system has marginalized — and one of the reasons Okonkwo and the elders find the church threatening.</p>

    <h2>The Internal Logic of Okonkwo's World</h2>

    <p>The Igbo world that Achebe renders in the novel's first section is not a simple or idealized world. It contains the <em>osu</em> system, the exile of twins, the killing of Ikemefuna, the subordination of women. Achebe does not sanitize any of this. He shows the world as it is.</p>

    <p>What he does insist on is that this world has internal coherence — that its practices, however difficult, follow from a consistent set of values and cosmological commitments. The Week of Peace is not arbitrary superstition; it is an expression of the community's dependence on Ala and its collective responsibility to protect the harvest. The egwugwu is not theatrical performance; it is the community's mechanism for accessing the authority of the accumulated dead. The title system is not mere vanity; it is the method by which a society without hereditary status determines who has earned the right to lead.</p>

    <p>Understanding this coherence is not the same as endorsing every practice it generates. But it is the prerequisite for understanding Achebe's fundamental assertion that precolonial Igboland was a world with philosophical depth, political sophistication, and genuine human achievement, not a darkness awaiting European light.</p>

